1075 #edb/ref 17592186547542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547935 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcc58af9-a17d-54ac-a6d0-c02b4698e66a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547942 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7d1b7f0-9375-5f6f-bc52-a79347709299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186547624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186547497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547960 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e947e310-d910-5ad9-bf8c-eecd137f0212" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=5,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=10,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=16$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=31.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29dbfdc5-a896-5333-926c-2c4e2687cdd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547991 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =16-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =31-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =10-3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159392" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.2,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c722f59-6d05-5224-b152-d5620b69f9be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547993 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.6-{1.2}^{2} \\\\  & =2.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(3)(0)f(3,0)+(1)(3)f(1,3)+(3)(3)f(3,3) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.8-2\\cdot 1.2 \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -0.6 \\\\ -0.6 & 2.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=2.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "498cfd6b-9969-58ee-9468-55dacd0e9142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547998 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.55$ | $0.25$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.8+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.2-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)f(2,1)+(3)(1)f(3,1)+(2)(2)f(2,2)+(3)(2)f(3,2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.55+3\\cdot 0.05+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =3.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.15-2.2\\cdot 1.4 \\\\  & =0.07.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.16 & 0.07 \\\\ 0.07 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}48y(x-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{4}{5},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{4},$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f28747a-e241-5f89-b726-699a85c63f84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548003 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48{y}^{3}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}(x{y}^{3}-{y}^{4})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{160}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{3}{320}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}48x{y}^{2}(x-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x/2}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-x{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{64}{x}^{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{5}{192}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{5}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{24}-(\\frac{4}{5})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{75} & \\frac{1}{120} \\\\ \\frac{1}{120} & \\frac{1}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159402" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53e9c56c-8467-523e-bd98-6ac7236cb57e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548007 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{4}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-{(0)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{33}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{5}-{(\\frac{5}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{80}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [-1,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{51}{80}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.6$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.8,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "67d9ef63-b4c5-5251-99ba-45e5490b628a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548011 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.8-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.3-1.6\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.24 & 0.02 \\\\ 0.02 & 0.16\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{16}({x}^{2}-y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{3},$ $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{20}{21},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{40}{27},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7039fb00-3d4a-54d6-a4b0-1bcf58d49ef2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548016 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\text{Var}[X].$\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{4}-y{x}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[y{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{6}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{32}{[\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{7}-{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{Var}[Y].$ Using the given results, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{27}-{(\\frac{20}{21})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{760}{1\\,323}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}xy\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\frac{5}{16}xy({x}^{2}-y)\\,\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{2})\\text{d}y\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}x]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{7})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{7}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{96}{[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{8}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}-(\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot (\\frac{20}{21}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{63}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{5}{63} \\\\ \\frac{5}{63} & \\frac{760}{1\\,323}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=6,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=7,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=40,$ and variances $\\text{Var}[X]=2$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=5.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72d6fd40-f077-555f-a2ea-48615443e754" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548018 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nUsing the above formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =40-6\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548023 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548023 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548023 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548023 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & b \\\\ c & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ is given above. Find $a+b+c.$" 