}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=1$\n\nTherefore, $a=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{8}{5},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9895539f-08a9-52d3-aba6-0550c7a4d108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548033 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we compute $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}(\\frac{1}{2}x{y}^{3})\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{32}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}-{(\\frac{8}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle\n\n$$\nS=[0,1]\\times [0,2].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=2x$ and $h(y)=\\frac{{y}^{3}}{4}.$\n\nHence, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{8}{75}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=1.7$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=0.6,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0df8fdd-91c9-54e0-8d8a-27e731147330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548042 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =3.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =3.1-{1.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.6-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(0)f(1,0)+(2)(0)f(2,0)+(1)(1)f(1,1)+(2)(1)f(2,1) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1-1.7\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =-0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.21 & -0.02 \\\\ -0.02 & 0.24\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=3,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=1,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=4,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=10$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=4.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cae81e4a-bed5-56e8-841e-be25ff76e753" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548053 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the 