$j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{31}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{1}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{13}=0$\n\n- $b={Σ}_{32}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{3},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]={Σ}_{23}=2$\n\nTherefore, $a+b=0+2=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159371" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=6,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=9$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=17.$ Find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea8dc2eb-77b6-5a6b-972c-806dd20059a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548072 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =9-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =17-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow lets find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =6-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & a \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe covariance matrix $Σ$ for some random variables ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ is given above. Find $a.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef075455-3a78-5d07-aadc-6948b6898570" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548074 #edb/ref 1074 "For random variables ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n},$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{n}] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Var}[{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{n}] \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{1}] & \\text{Cov}[{X}_{n},{X}_{2}] & ⋯ & \\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThat is,\n\n- the $i$th diagonal element is ${Σ}_{ii}=\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}],$ and\n\n- the element in the $i$th row and $j$th column for $i\\ne j$ is ${Σ}_{ij}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{Cov}[Y,X].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the covariance matrix is always symmetric (moreover, it is a positive semi-definite matrix). \n\nSo, we can find the missing elements as follows:\n\n- $a={Σ}_{12}=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{1}]={Σ}_{21}=3$\n\nTherefore, $a=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=5$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.7,$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f655f9f5-f0a0-522e-aa7b-2faf26b5e6dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548077 #edb/ref 1074 "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the covariance matrix $Σ$ is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ | $0.75$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a support ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =28-{5}^{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a support ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.65+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =8.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =8.2-{2.7}^{2} \\\\  & =0.91.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ By the rule of the lazy statistician, we find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(2)f(2,2)+(6)(2)f(6,2)+(2)(4)f(2,4)+(6)(4)f(6,4) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0.15+12\\cdot 0.5+8\\cdot 0.1+24\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =13.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =13.4-5\\cdot 2.7 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nΣ=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & \\text{Cov}[X,Y] \\\\ \\text{Cov}[Y,X] & \\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0.91\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
